[#:seon.task{:name
            "every-form-is-analyzed-because-its-result-handle-is-a-binding",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "seon.turn.batch/derive-form runs clj-kondo (analyze-form -> seon.fn/runtime-analysis-batch) for every evaluated form, including ones that define nothing: publication interns the value under its result handle (`result/e...`, seon.sci.eval/bind-result!), the write observer reports that :bind :root write, and derive-form's `finals` does not drop the result namespace the way seon.turn/entry-bindings does. Reproduce: MCP eval_clj (sci, agent r-read, default 722f110df, 2026-10-07) of forty forms (+ 0 1) ... (+ 39 1): seon.turn.batch/analyze-form 40 calls, 441 ms of a 667 ms evaluate-batch; read-row 41 calls 3.9 ms; segments 0.45 ms (profile cell deltas). Correct: only a form whose writes bind a program Var is analyzed; a plain call costs no analysis. Proof: a test running (+ 1 2) through the agent entrance asserts its batch entry carries no analysis and records no program change.",
            :must-read
            #{seon.sci.eval/bind-result!
              seon.turn/entry-bindings
              seon.turn.batch/derive-form},
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-read-notes.md"}}
